course: adult-life
lesson: 17
title: Gran, cousins and the whole wide family
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your family is bigger than the people in your house: grandparents, aunts, uncles, cousins, and relatives by marriage called in-laws.
explain: A big family brings warmth and noise, and also old quarrels. Some relatives get on, some do not, and a few stop speaking for years.
explain: In Cork, twenty-three people squeeze into Gran's house for Christmas. There are not enough chairs, Uncle Rob is missing because of a row, and Aunt Pam says "Blood is thicker than water" over the trifle.
explain: Grown-ups also tease children about girlfriends and crushes, and older people sometimes find new partners late in life. These are ordinary parts of a big family.
fact: Some children live with their grandparents, who raise them when the parents cannot, and that is a real family.
fact: Dad's mother is Mum's mother-in-law, a relative by marriage, and in-laws do not always find long visits easy.
fact: Big family gatherings bring together relatives who rarely meet, and they can be fun and stressful at the same time.
fact: "We don't talk to that side of the family" means there was a falling-out, a feud, and some relatives stopped speaking.
fact: Family feuds often start over money, a will or hurtful words, and they can last for years.
fact: Cousins are the children of your aunts and uncles, and families spread out, so some cousins only meet at weddings and funerals.
fact: When a grown-up asks "Has he got a little girlfriend?", they are usually joking, and you do not have to answer.
fact: A crush means really liking someone and thinking about them a lot; it is a private feeling and often passes.
fact: A golden wedding anniversary means a couple has been married for fifty years.
fact: "Blood is thicker than water" is a saying that means family bonds are stronger than other bonds.
fact: Sometimes a relative is left out of a family event after an argument the grown-ups think is private.
fact: An older person who finds a new partner after losing a husband or wife has not forgotten the one who died.
exercise: Your friend lives with their grandparents. Which is true? | It is a real family; grandparents sometimes raise children | It is only for the school holidays, not a real home for the friend | Grandparents are not allowed to look after children | It means your friend has been very naughty
exercise: Dad says, "My mother's coming for a week," and Mum goes quiet. Why might she? | Long visits from in-laws can be tiring for her | Mum has never met Dad's mother before and is shy about it | Mum wants Gran to stay for a month | Gran is coming to live with them for good
exercise: What is Dad's mother to Mum? | Her mother-in-law | Her step-mother | Her aunt | Her grandmother
exercise: Christmas at Gran's with twenty-three people and not enough chairs. Which is true about big gatherings? | They can be fun and stressful at once | They are always calm and quiet | Grown-ups never argue at them | Only children enjoy them at all
exercise: Mum says, "We don't talk to that side of the family." What does it mean? | There was a falling-out and they stopped speaking | That side of the family speaks another language at home | They live too far away for phone calls | They talk only by letters and cards
exercise: What often starts a family feud? | Money, a will or hurtful words | The weather at a family barbecue | Which TV channel to watch | The colour of the wedding cake
exercise: Who are your cousins? | The children of your aunts and uncles | The brothers and sisters of your parents | Your parents' oldest friends | Children who live on your street
exercise: Why do some cousins only meet at weddings and funerals? | Families spread out and lives get busy | Cousins are not allowed to visit each other's houses | It is a rule that cousins meet twice a year | Cousins only exist at big events
exercise: A grown-up at a party asks, "Has he got a little girlfriend at school?" What can you do? | Not answer, or say "I've got lots of friends" | Tell them every secret about your class | Tell them to shut up and walk off | Make up a name so they stop asking
exercise: A classmate says she "fancies" someone. What does it mean? | She has a crush and really likes him | She wants to dress up as him | She thinks he is good at fancy dress | She is angry with him today
exercise: Gran and Grandad are having a "golden wedding". What is it? | Fifty years of marriage | Their wedding with gold rings | A wedding in a golden church | Twenty-five years of marriage
exercise: Aunt Pam says, "Blood is thicker than water." What does she mean? | Family bonds are stronger than other bonds | Someone has cut themselves badly | Water is good for your blood | Family members all share exactly one blood group
exercise: Uncle Rob isn't invited to the barbecue this year. What is the most likely reason? | The grown-ups have fallen out with him | He is allergic to barbecue food | He is the one cooking at another party | He forgot to buy a ticket for it
exercise: You miss Uncle Rob, who was left out. What can you do? | Tell a parent that you miss him | Pretend you never knew him | Shout at Gran about it at the party | Invite him secretly without asking
exercise: Grandad has a "lady friend" a few years after Gran died. Which is true? | He can be glad of company and still remember Gran | It means he has forgotten all about Gran and their life together | Older people are not allowed new partners | It is a secret you must tell the whole street
exercise: A classmate's crush gets teased by the class. Which is right? | Crushes are private and should not be teased | Crushes are silly and must be laughed at by everyone in the class | Only grown-ups are allowed to have crushes | You must tell the teacher who she likes
test: Overheard: Mum on the phone, "Christmas at your mother's again? Fine. FINE." What is going on? | Mum is not keen on another long visit to her in-law | Mum loves the idea and is cheering | Mum is booking a hotel for the holidays | Mum is talking to her own mother
test: Your cousin says, "Gran's sister's kids are our cousins too, but like, second ones." What is she talking about? | Second cousins, more distant relatives | Step-siblings from Gran's marriage | Half-siblings who share one parent | Twins from the same family
test: Your brother says, "Blood is thicker than water means blood is heavier, that's science." Put him right. | It is a saying: family bonds are the strongest | He is right, it is only about science | It means you should drink more water | It means families never ever argue
test: Gossip at the barbecue: "Did you see who's not here? After what he said at the funeral, can you blame them?" What is being said? | A relative was left out after hurtful words | A relative is ill in hospital and could not come | A relative has gone on holiday abroad | A relative is late because of traffic
test: Uncle Kev, laughing: "Ooh, got your eye on anyone at school, eh? Eh?" You go red. What is he doing? | Teasing in fun, and you need not answer | Asking for a list of the class | Telling you off for being rude | Asking which school you will go to next
test: Mum, sighing: "That lot haven't spoken since Nana's will." What most likely happened? | The family fell out over what was left in a will | Nana wrote them all a long letter | The family went abroad and lost touch | They speak only by text since then
test: An Australian girl: "I live with Nan and Pop, Mum can't look after me right now." What is true? | Her grandparents are raising her, and it is a real family | She is only visiting for a short holiday | Her grandparents must give her back by law when she turns ten | She has no family at all now
test: Mum, reading a card: "Fifty years! I can't imagine putting up with anyone that long." What is it for? | A golden wedding anniversary | A fiftieth birthday party | A silver wedding anniversary | A retirement party
test: Your sister says, "Grandad's got a girlfriend, so he doesn't love Gran any more." Put her right. | He can find company and still remember Gran | She is right, he has forgotten Gran | She is right, he must stop seeing her | Grandad is not allowed to have friends
test: Dad at a funeral: "Haven't seen these two since your cousin Shona's wedding." Why so long? | Relatives spread out and rarely meet | Cousins are kept apart on purpose | Funerals are the only place cousins can go | They had a big feud about the wedding
test: Gran, crossly: "I don't care what he said, he's still family and he's coming." What does Gran believe? | Family should stick together despite rows | Nobody should come to family parties | Only friends should be invited to parties | Rows should end with someone left out
exam: Which of the following designations correctly characterises the mother of one's spouse? | Mother-in-law | Step-mother | Godmother | Foster mother
exam: Identify the duration of marriage commemorated by a golden wedding anniversary. | Fifty years | Twenty-five years | Forty years | Sixty years
exam: Select the response most consistent with the established meaning of the proverb "Blood is thicker than water". | Ties of kinship are stronger than other ties | Physical health is of greater importance than thirst | Water ought not to be mixed with other liquids | Relatives ought never to quarrel with one another
exam: It is given that a widowed grandparent has subsequently formed a new companionship. Which statement is consistent with the established facts? | The new companionship does not signify that the deceased spouse is forgotten | The new companionship is prohibited by law | The new companionship signifies that the grandparent has ceased to grieve entirely | The new companionship requires the consent of the grandchildren
